{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"An adult central nervous system tumor is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissues of the brain and/or spinal cord.","source_span":"An adult central nervous system tumor is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissues of the brain and/or spinal cord."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"A tumor that starts in another part of the body and spreads to the brain is called a metastatic brain tumor.","source_span":"A tumor that starts in another part of the body and spreads to the brain is called a metastatic brain tumor."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"Up to half of metastatic brain tumors originate from lung cancer.","source_span":"Up to half of metastatic brain tumors are from lung cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"The most common cancers that spread to the leptomeninges (leptomeningeal carcinomatosis) are breast cancer, lung cancer, leukemia, and lymphoma.","source_span":"The most common cancers that spread to the leptomeninges include: -  Breast cancer.    - Lung cancer.    -  Leukemia.    -  Lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"The cerebrum, the largest part of the brain, controls thinking, learning, problem solving, emotions, speech, reading, writing, and voluntary movement.","source_span":"The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain. It is at the top of the head. The cerebrum controls thinking, learning, problem solving, emotions, speech, reading, writing, and voluntary movement."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"The brain stem controls breathing, heart rate, and the nerves and muscles used to see, hear, walk, talk, and eat.","source_span":"The brain stem controls breathing, heart rate, and the nerves and muscles used to see, hear, walk, talk, and eat."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"WHO Grade I brain tumors may be cured if they are completely removed by surgery.","source_span":"Grade I brain tumors may be cured if they are completely removed by surgery."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","statement":"The cerebellum, located in the lower back of the brain, controls movement, balance, and posture.","source_span":"The cerebellum is in the lower back of the brain (near the middle of the back of the head). It controls movement, balance, and posture."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What is a metastatic brain tumor?","answer":"A metastatic brain tumor is a tumor that starts in another part of the body and spreads to the brain."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What proportion of metastatic brain tumors originate from lung cancer?","answer":"Up to half of metastatic brain tumors are from lung cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which cancers most commonly spread to the leptomeninges?","answer":"Breast cancer, lung cancer, leukemia, and lymphoma are the most common cancers that spread to the leptomeninges."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What functions does the cerebrum control?","answer":"The cerebrum, the largest part of the brain, controls thinking, learning, problem solving, emotions, speech, reading, writing, and voluntary movement."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What bodily functions does the brain stem control?","answer":"The brain stem controls breathing, heart rate, and the nerves and muscles used to see, hear, walk, talk, and eat."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the outlook for a WHO Grade I brain tumor if it is completely removed by surgery?","answer":"Grade I brain tumors may be cured if they are completely removed by surgery."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","statement":"A childhood brain or spinal cord tumor is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissues of the brain or spinal cord.","source_span":"A childhood brain or spinal cord tumor is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissues of the brain or spinal cord."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","statement":"Brain and spinal cord tumors are the third most common type of childhood cancer, after leukemia and lymphoma.","source_span":"brain and spinal cord tumors are the third most common type of childhood cancer, after leukemia and lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","statement":"The cause of most childhood brain and spinal cord tumors is unknown.","source_span":"The cause of most childhood brain and spinal cord tumors is unknown."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","statement":"Brain tumor signs and symptoms in children include morning headache or headache that goes away after vomiting, frequent nausea and vomiting, vision/hearing/speech problems, loss of balance, unusual sleepiness, personality changes, seizures, and increase in head size in infants.","source_span":"Morning headache or headache that goes away after vomiting.    - Frequent nausea and vomiting.     - Vision, hearing, and speech problems.    - Loss of balance and trouble walking.    - Unusual sleepiness or change in activity level.     - Unusual changes in personality or behavior.    -  Seizures.    - Increase in the head size (in infants)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","statement":"Spinal cord tumor signs and symptoms in children include back pain or pain spreading toward the arms or legs, a change in bowel habits or trouble urinating, weakness in the legs, and trouble walking.","source_span":"Back pain or pain that spreads from the back towards the arms or legs.    - A change in bowel habits or trouble urinating.    - Weakness in the legs.    - Trouble walking."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","statement":"MRI with gadolinium uses a magnet, radio waves, and a computer to make detailed pictures of the brain and spinal cord, with gadolinium injected into a vein so cancer cells show up brighter; it is also called nuclear magnetic resonance imaging (NMRI).","source_span":"MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) with gadolinium : A procedure that uses a magnet, radio waves, and a computer to make a series of detailed pictures of the brain and spinal cord. A substance called gadolinium is injected into a vein. The gadolinium collects around the cancer cells so they show up brighter in the picture. This procedure is also called nuclear magnetic resonance imaging (NMRI)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","statement":"A neurological exam is a series of questions and tests that check the brain, spinal cord, and nerve function, including mental status, coordination, ability to walk normally, and how well the muscles, senses, and reflexes work.","source_span":"Neurological exam : A series of questions and tests to check the brain, spinal cord, and nerve function. The exam checks a persons mental status, coordination, and ability to walk normally, and how well the muscles, senses, and reflexes work."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Where do brain and spinal cord tumors rank among childhood cancers, and which cancers are more common?","answer":"Brain and spinal cord tumors are the third most common type of childhood cancer, after leukemia and lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the known cause of most childhood brain and spinal cord tumors?","answer":"The cause of most childhood brain and spinal cord tumors is unknown."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are some signs and symptoms of a brain tumor in a child?","answer":"Signs include morning headache or headache that goes away after vomiting, frequent nausea and vomiting, vision/hearing/speech problems, loss of balance, unusual sleepiness, personality changes, seizures, and increased head size in infants."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are signs and symptoms of a spinal cord tumor in a child?","answer":"Signs include back pain or pain spreading toward the arms or legs, a change in bowel habits or trouble urinating, weakness in the legs, and trouble walking."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How does MRI with gadolinium help detect childhood brain and spinal cord tumors, and what is another name for the procedure?","answer":"Gadolinium is injected into a vein and collects around cancer cells so they appear brighter on the MRI's detailed pictures of the brain and spinal cord; the procedure is also called nuclear magnetic resonance imaging (NMRI)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What does a neurological exam check for in a child suspected of having a brain or spinal cord tumor?","answer":"It checks brain, spinal cord, and nerve function, including mental status, coordination, ability to walk normally, and how well the muscles, senses, and reflexes work."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","statement":"Astrocytomas are tumors that start in star-shaped brain cells called astrocytes.","source_span":"Astrocytomas are tumors that start in star-shaped brain cells called astrocytes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","statement":"An astrocyte is a type of glial cell, and astrocytoma is a type of glioma.","source_span":"An astrocyte is a type of glial cell... An astrocytoma is a type of glioma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","statement":"Astrocytoma is the most common type of glioma diagnosed in children.","source_span":"Astrocytoma is the most common type of glioma diagnosed in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","statement":"Possible risk factors for astrocytoma include past radiation therapy to the brain and having certain genetic disorders such as neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) or tuberous sclerosis.","source_span":"Possible risk factors for astrocytoma include:          - Past radiation therapy to the brain.    - Having certain genetic disorders, such as neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) or tuberous sclerosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","statement":"The hypothalamus, located in the middle of the base of the brain, controls body temperature, hunger, and thirst.","source_span":"Hypothalamus : The area in the middle of the base of the brain. It controls body temperature, hunger, and thirst."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","statement":"Astrocytomas are most common in the cerebrum, cerebellum, brain stem, hypothalamus, visual pathway, and spinal cord.","source_span":"Astrocytomas are most common in these parts of the central nervous system (CNS):         -   Cerebrum ... -   Cerebellum ... -   Brain stem ... -   Hypothalamus ... -  Visual pathway... -  Spinal cord"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","statement":"A visual field exam measures both central vision and peripheral vision, testing each eye separately while the other eye is covered.","source_span":"This test measures both central vision (how much a person can see when looking straight ahead) and peripheral vision (how much a person can see in all other directions while staring straight ahead). The eyes are tested one at a time. The eye not being tested is covered."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What are astrocytomas and what type of cell do they originate from?","answer":"Astrocytomas are tumors that start in star-shaped brain cells called astrocytes, which are a type of glial cell; astrocytoma is itself a type of glioma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the most common type of glioma diagnosed in children?","answer":"Astrocytoma is the most common type of glioma diagnosed in children."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are two possible risk factors for childhood astrocytoma?","answer":"Possible risk factors include past radiation therapy to the brain and having certain genetic disorders such as neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) or tuberous sclerosis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What functions does the hypothalamus control?","answer":"The hypothalamus, located in the middle of the base of the brain, controls body temperature, hunger, and thirst."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","fact_refs":[5],"question":"In which parts of the central nervous system are astrocytomas most commonly found?","answer":"Astrocytomas are most common in the cerebrum, cerebellum, brain stem, hypothalamus, visual pathway, and spinal cord."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000006_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What does a visual field exam measure and how is it conducted?","answer":"A visual field exam measures both central vision and peripheral vision, testing each eye separately while the other eye is covered."}
